<?xml version="1.0"?>
<CP_Scriptfile date="Feb 20, 2023">
<CPscript title="Multi-connected Refinement" level="min" screenmode="map" >
<description>Discrete conformal maps of multi-connected domains to circle domains converge under refinement to their classical counterpart. This script follows up on "CircleDomains.xmd" and again requires user intervention.</description>
<text> We revisit discrete conformal maps of the 4-connected region from "CircleDomains.xmd" displayed in p1. We compare the earlier packing to a refined version and code for quasiconformal distortion.

(NOTE: Due to a programming problem with 'cookie', this script is preliminary. A future version will allow you to create different plane regions, choose the overlay packings, set the refinement level, etc. For now we restrict to prepared examples, though some user intervention is required.) </text>
<cmd name="*" mnemonic="*" iconname="dnd_multi.png">act 1;Cleanse;infile_read hex_overlay.p;[0];[1];Map 0 1 </cmd>
<Section title="The Coarse Construction">
<text> We start with the region cut from a coarse packing. (This is where the defined paths and 'cookie' would be applied in place of using a prepared packing..) </text>
<cmd iconname="psi.png">act 1;infile_read threehole.p;Disp -w -c -cfc50 b -g;[0];[1];[2];[3]; </cmd>
<text> You must press 'a' on circles in any 3 of the 4 boundary components. An ideal vertex will be add and a spray of new edges from the origin appears. The important thing is that now we have a simply connected complex.

Go ahead, choose the 3 boundary components. The next command then puts the maximal packing in p0 and tosses out the added ideal vertices. It's preferrable to think of our map as going from the original domain in p1 to the circle domain in p0. You can click circles to compare. </text>
<cmd iconname="xeyes.png">act 0;copy -p1 0;alpha M;max_pack;disp -w -cc5t4 M;puncture M;disp -cc5t4 M;puncture M;disp -cc5t4 M;puncture M;puncture -p1 M;puncture -p1 M;puncture -p1 M;set_disp_text -w -c -cfc50 b;disp -c;Disp -p1 -w -c -cfc50 b; </cmd>
<text> Press 'k' and the faces of p0 will be color coded for quasiconformal dilatation. The distribution of blue faces, those with more distortion, is perhaps what you might have expected -- boundary bends and thin necks. </text>
</Section>
<Section title="A Refinement">
<text> You might use "Screendump" to save an image of the domain and color coded range for later comparison. Now we carry out the same construction with a refined overlay. </text>
<cmd iconname="netbeans.png">act 1;infile_read fine3.p;Disp -w -c -cfc50 b -g;[0];[1];[2];[3]; </cmd>
<text> Again, press 'a' to choose 3 boundary components, preferrably the same you used at the coarse stage. After you have those, then next command puts the domain in p0. </text>
<cmd iconname="klines.png">act 0;copy -p1 0;alpha M;max_pack 25000;disp -w -cc5t4 M;puncture M;disp -cc5t4 M;puncture M;disp -cc5t4 M;puncture M;puncture -p1 M;puncture -p1 M;puncture -p1 M;set_disp_text -w -c -cfc50 b;disp -c;Disp -p1 -w -c -cfc50 b; </cmd>
<text> Press 'k' to see the new distortion, and perhaps do another Screendump. 

It is known that the discrete analytic maps from p0 to p1 converge uniformly on compact subsets of out region to a classical analytic function to a circle domain in the unit disc. In comparing the two refinement levels, there are (at least) two things to watch: 

(1) Quasiconformal distortion: As refinement level goes up, distortion fades to white on compact subsets of the image region. This reflects the fact that the discrete functions are converging locally to analytic functions.

(2) The circle domains: But you also have to compare the circle domains, which are only determined up to automorphisms of the unit disc. I have imposed a normalization so that the circle associated with the last boundary component you chose with 'a' would be centered at the origin. This should help in comparing the two experiments. In fact, with appropriate normalization, the discrete circle domains do converge to the classical circle domain. 

When I have 'cookie' working correctly, you will have more flexibility with the construction illustrated here. </text>
</Section>
<Section title="Tools">
<cmd inline="no" name="k" mnemonic="k" tooltip="color code by quasiconformal dilatation" iconname="kivio.png">copy -p0 2;geom_to_e -p2;color -p1 -f qc -q2 -m 1.2 a;disp -p1 -w -ffc -g;[0];[1];[2];[3]; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="a" mnemonic="a" tooltip="tool for choosing boundary component for ideal vertex" iconname="kuickshow.png">set_vlist z #XY;add_ideal vlist[0];set_rad .01 M;disp -e Iv M;set_Vlist Vlist M; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="0" mnemonic="0" tooltip="display outer curve" iconname="xeyes.png">infile_path bubble.g;disp -p1 -g; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="1" mnemonic="1" tooltip="display curve 1" iconname="kivio.png">infile_path hole1.g;disp -p1 -g; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="2" mnemonic="2" tooltip="display curve 2" iconname="kuickshow.png">infile_path hole2.g;disp -p1 -g; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="3" mnemonic="3" tooltip="display curve 3" iconname="mozilla.png">infile_path hole3.g;disp -p1 -g; </cmd>
</Section>
  </CPscript>
